{
  "original_id": "newwin-00001-p05",
  "archive_id": "BW2-N01-P05",
  "title": "Maximal sorting time under clumped-letter insertion in two short multiplicity profiles",
  "batch_label": "新WIN-1批次",
  "kind": "result-note",
  "original_status": "unresolved",
  "scope": "Let phi be the greedy aba-avoiding stack map, and let T be its sorting time. For every integer r>=3, let q be any word using exactly r letters, each occurring twice except that at most one letter may occur three times. Let W be any word whose deletion of a set A of additional letters equals q, with every letter of A occurring in one contiguous nonempty block in W. There is no bound on |A| or on those block multiplicities. Then T(W)=r if and only if T(q)=r. The proof uses a complete 28,800-input computer-assisted base at r=3 and induction on r. In particular, for every run-free q on r>=3 letters, each occurring at least twice, with 2r<=|q|<=2r+1, and every new terminal letter x, T(qx)=r iff T(q)=r. This proves the original first-letter deletion bridge for k>=4; it does not provide the requested complete uniform classification or enumeration M_k of length-2k+2 inputs.",
  "gate_basis": "Current released terminal, manifest, PDF, TeX freeze, snapshot files, provenance and four audits agree.",
